lessons.body:
NestJS DI Tokens and Module Binding
Volume 07 - Advanced Application Architecture | Phase 24

Interfaces disappear at runtime
TypeScript interfaces are compile-time only, so NestJS cannot inject TaskRepository directly. The
runtime container needs a token. Current NestJS documentation explicitly supports strings,
symbols, enums, classes, and abstract classes as provider tokens. TaskFlow chooses a Symbol to
avoid accidental collisions.

Token and provider
export const TASK_REPOSITORY =
Symbol("TASK_REPOSITORY")

@Module({
providers: [
TasksService,
{
provide: TASK_REPOSITORY,
useClass: PrismaTaskRepository,
},
],
})
export class TasksModule {}

Inject the contract
constructor(
@Inject(TASK_REPOSITORY)
private readonly tasks: TaskRepository,
) {}

Contract separates what the service needs from how Prisma performs it

TasksService                            DI token                  PrismaTaskRepository
TaskRepository contract                 TASK_REPOSITORY                implements contract

PrismaService
generated client + adapter

Figure 24.1 - Inject the contract

Why useClass matters
The service depends on the token and interface shape, while the module decides which concrete
implementation satisfies that dependency. Tests can supply a fake with useValue without

changing production code.

