lessons.body:
Unit Testing with a Fake Repository
Volume 07 - Advanced Application Architecture | Phase 24

The repository pays rent in tests
NestJS makes provider replacement straightforward. Instead of teaching a service test every
Prisma method and nested relation shape, we can inject a tiny fake that implements the
repository contract.

In-memory fake
class FakeTaskRepository implements TaskRepository {
items = new Map<string, TaskRecord>()

async findById(id: string) {
return this.items.get(id) ?? null
}

async update(id: string, input: UpdateTaskRecord) {
const current = this.items.get(id)
if (!current) throw new Error("missing fake record")

const next = { ...current, ...input }
this.items.set(id, next)
return next
}

// implement only the contract, not Prisma
}

Nest testing module
const moduleRef = await Test.createTestingModule({
providers: [
TasksService,
{
provide: TASK_REPOSITORY,
useValue: fakeRepository,
},
],
}).compile()

What this test proves
• Status-transition rules.

• 403/404 domain decisions.

• Derived completedAt behavior.

• Due-date policy.

• Service orchestration independent of PostgreSQL.

What it does not prove
A fake repository does not prove that Prisma relation filters, indexes, migrations, constraints, or
SQL behavior are correct. Those belong to repository integration tests.

